    "faq_shrimp": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_done": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when the bites are done?",
            "text": "<p>400&deg;F for six to eight minutes, flipping once halfway, but go by the corners rather than the timer. They colour before the flat faces do, and once they are properly golden the centre is cooked. A fork slid into the thickest cube should separate the flakes without resistance. Because the cubes are small they carry on cooking for a moment after they leave the basket, so pull them at golden rather than waiting for dark.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_dry": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why does the fish have to be patted dry?",
            "text": "<p>Because a wet surface steams instead of browning. An air fryer works by moving hot dry air fast over the food, and any moisture sitting on the cubes has to boil off before the surface can colour &mdash; by the time it does, the inside is overcooked. Pat every side with paper towel after cutting, and if you have the time leave the cubes uncovered in the fridge while you make the sauce. That is the whole trick to the crust.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_crowd": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I pile the bites into the basket?",
            "text": "<p>No &mdash; that is the other way to lose the crust. The cubes need space around them for the air to move, so lay them out in a single layer with a gap between each one. Two smaller batches will beat one crowded one every time, and the first batch holds its crisp while the second cooks.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_sauce": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Should I toss the bites in the tzatziki?",
            "text": "<p>Put it on at the table, not in the basket. The sauce is cold, thick and yogurt-based, and anything that crisped in the air fryer goes soft the moment it sits in it. Spoon it over the assembled bowl, or serve it alongside so people can dip.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_avocado": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will the avocado sauce go brown?",
            "text": "<p>Not overnight. There is enough lemon juice in it to hold the colour, and blending it into yogurt limits how much air reaches the avocado. Keep it in the fridge with cling film pressed down onto the surface rather than stretched over the top of the bowl, which is what leaves an air gap.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I thaw the steelhead?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours, then pat it dry before cutting. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of cold water and change the water every 20 minutes. Never thaw fish on the counter or in warm water, and never thaw it out of the pack &mdash; it takes on water and that works directly against the crust.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_done",
        "q_dry",
        "q_crowd",
        "q_sauce",
        "q_avocado",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
